## Script

**Diagnosis.** The test is flaky because both sides of the assertion read the real clock at two slightly different moments:

```js
expect(dueDate(new Date())).toEqual(addDays(new Date(), 30))
```

If the second `new Date()` lands on the next calendar day (e.g. 23:59:59.999 vs 00:00:00.001), the expected value is one day later than the actual, and the test fails. It also isn't really testing the rule — the "expected" side re-implements the same +30-day logic it's meant to verify. A test must not depend on when it runs.

**Fix.** Pin the issue date to a literal, and assert against a literal expected date computed by hand.

## Corrected test file — `src/billing/due.test.js`

```js
const { dueDate } = require('./due');

describe('dueDate', () => {
  test('is 30 days after the issue date', () => {
    // 15 Jan 2024, local time (built from parts, not a string, to avoid UTC parsing)
    const issueDate = new Date(2024, 0, 15);

    // 15 Jan + 30 days = 14 Feb 2024
    expect(dueDate(issueDate)).toEqual(new Date(2024, 1, 14));
  });

  test('is 30 days after an issue date at the end of a month', () => {
    // 31 Jan 2024 (leap year) + 30 days = 1 Mar 2024
    expect(dueDate(new Date(2024, 0, 31))).toEqual(new Date(2024, 2, 1));
  });

  test('does not mutate the issue date passed in', () => {
    const issueDate = new Date(2024, 0, 15);
    dueDate(issueDate);
    expect(issueDate).toEqual(new Date(2024, 0, 15));
  });
});
```

## Why this is right

- **No real clock.** Every date is a literal, so the test gives the same result at 09:00 and at 23:59:59.
- **Local-time construction.** `new Date(2024, 0, 15)` builds the date from parts in local time, matching how `dueDate` will be handed and return dates. Writing `new Date('2024-01-15')` would parse as UTC midnight and can shift a day in negative-offset zones — a different flavour of the same bug.
- **Independent expectation.** 14 Feb 2024 is worked out by hand, not by calling `addDays(...)`, so the test can actually catch a wrong implementation instead of agreeing with it.
- **Extra coverage.** Month-end/leap-year boundary and the "don't mutate the caller's Date" guard (implementations using `issueDate.setDate(...)` in place are a common bug).

