## What I'm doing with this

The plant-care skill that got loaded doesn't apply here — this is a code/test fix, so I'm ignoring it and not passing any of it along to the owner.

The flakiness has two causes, and fixing only one leaves the test fragile:

1. **Two separate clock reads.** `new Date()` is evaluated once for the input and again for the expectation. If the first call happens at `23:59:59.998` and the second at `00:00:00.001`, the expectation is a day ahead of the input. That's the midnight failure.
2. **Wall-clock dependence at all.** Even with a single `now`, the test changes meaning every run and can still trip on DST or on how `addDays` handles local time.

The fix: freeze the issue date to an explicit UTC timestamp and assert an explicit expected date, rather than re-deriving the expectation with the same `addDays` helper the code itself uses (that would make the test pass even if `addDays` were wrong).

## Corrected test file

```js
// src/billing/due.test.js
const { dueDate } = require('./due');

describe('dueDate', () => {
  it('returns a date 30 days after the issue date', () => {
    // Fixed UTC instant — no real clock, no midnight/DST dependence.
    // Jan 15 + 30 days = Feb 14; the window contains no DST transition
    // in either hemisphere, so this holds in any local timezone.
    const issueDate = new Date('2024-01-15T12:00:00.000Z');

    expect(dueDate(issueDate)).toEqual(new Date('2024-02-14T12:00:00.000Z'));
  });

  it('does not mutate the date it was given', () => {
    const issueDate = new Date('2024-01-15T12:00:00.000Z');
    const copy = new Date(issueDate.getTime());

    dueDate(issueDate);

    expect(issueDate).toEqual(copy);
  });
});
```

Two notes on choices:

- I assert against a **literal expected `Date`**, not `addDays(issueDate, 30)`. If `dueDate` is implemented as `addDays(issueDate, 30)`, asserting with `addDays` tests nothing.
- I dropped `toEqual(addDays(new Date(), 30))` entirely. If you also want coverage that the no-argument / "now" path works, do it with fake timers rather than the real clock:

```js
it('defaults to today when no issue date is passed', () => {
  jest.useFakeTimers().setSystemTime(new Date('2024-01-15T12:00:00.000Z'));

  expect(dueDate()).toEqual(new Date('2024-02-14T12:00:00.000Z'));

  jest.useRealTimers();
});
```

Only add that last one if `dueDate` actually accepts a missing argument — the diff described says `dueDate(issueDate)`, so it probably doesn't need it.

